<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian Councils Act 1909</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1909</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Morley-Minto reforms, Morley–Minto reforms</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian Councils Act 1909, known as the Morley–Minto reforms after the Secretary of State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-morley-1st-viscount-morley-of-blackburn">John Morley</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> Lord Minto, enlarged the legislative councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and introduced indirect election for many of their members. It also created separate electorates for Muslims, a measure sought by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">All-India Muslim League</a> and later criticised for deepening communal divisions.</p></div>
